WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:07.640
Dar Dixon, Actor: I wanted to get a day job
(this was 20 some odd years ago).

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.400
I’d had a business with my folks.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:11.720
I was a small print and copy shop.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:17.660
I knew nothing about it but I took all the
failures of my other business and did the

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.119
opposite of that and did really well with
it and we were able to raise the business

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:27.760
up quite a bit and drive and make it much
more profitable and my folks were able to

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.290
sell off and retire.

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:32.810
And so I thought I’ll go see about this
copier job.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:38.520
I went to this company (it shall remain nameless)
and for an interview I walked in and she said

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.149
“Just fill out the application.

00:00:40.149 --> 00:00:41.589
Mr. X will see you shortly.”

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:44.640
And I said “Oh, that’s okay I don’t
fill out the application.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.270
I’ve already got my C.V. with me.”

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.629
She just kind of looked at me.

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:58.019
I sat down, waited and had a chance to go
sit down with Mr. X (really nice man).

00:00:58.019 --> 00:00:59.179
The job was just terrible.

00:00:59.179 --> 00:01:01.239
They were going to give me the worst (I knew
what they were going to do) they were going

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:07.130
to give me the worst territory and it’s
going to be business-to-business sales and

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:13.420
I’m going to try to get them to buy copiers
from this company and the pay was going to

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.990
be nothing.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:18.380
There wasn’t going to be a car allowance
or cell phone allowance or laptop allowance

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:24.570
or insurance or any benefits or medical or
dental or anything and I was like “I get

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.570
it I know the drill.”

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:29.220
But I’ll show my worth and things will change.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.689
We’d done this listening exercise in an
acting class I was doing at the time and it

00:01:35.689 --> 00:01:42.729
was really mind-blowing when we did it (for
me it was) because I had an experience with

00:01:42.729 --> 00:01:48.799
the actress that I was working with that night
and I just watched this whole shift occur

00:01:48.799 --> 00:01:49.799
in her.

00:01:49.799 --> 00:01:51.320
I didn’t say anything.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:56.409
She was just talking and I was just listening
with 100 percent focus.

00:01:56.409 --> 00:02:02.229
Which by the way, just doing that, it’s
exhausting the first time you do it.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:05.710
But it was also really interesting and liberating.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:09.610
I thought I’m going to try that today.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:15.980
So I’m sitting in this interview and we
say a few pleasantries back and forth and

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:17.230
he says “Well tell me about yourself?”

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:22.060
And I go “Well there’s a lot I could say
about myself but I would really like to hear

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.050
what your biggest challenges are?

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:25.050
Who has been great at this job?

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.160
Who has not been great at this job?

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.700
Anything you can share about that would be
really helpful to me.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.410
I don’t want to waste your time.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.280
I certainly wouldn’t dream of wasting your
time.”

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.170
I think that was like the totality of what
I said.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.890
And then I didn’t say another word for 45
minutes.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.530
And he just started to talk.

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.710
I could see in his mind “What the heck is
this?”

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:52.710
Right?

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:53.710
Film Courage: Who is this man?

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:55.620
Dar: Yes, who is this guy, right?

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.440
Which it was actually a really smart movie.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:02.500
I wasn’t trying to be smart or anything
like that or saying I’m necessarily smart.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:09.110
But all I did for the next 45 minutes is “Mm-hmm…Oh
really?…Wow.”

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.400
But I was actively listening.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.150
There are things you can do (I won’t get
into it here) that you train yourself to actively

00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:22.040
listen and eye contact (total focus) and I’m
literally hearing everything you are saying

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.290
and I did that for 45 minutes with this guy.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:30.090
He just kept opening up more and more and
a smile came across his face about five minutes

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.780
in and it wouldn’t leave.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:36.030
20 minutes in he’s telling us funny stories
and anecdotes about his family and stuff and

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:39.800
it just kept on going and at about the 45
minute mark (I remember that well) he goes

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:40.800
“You know what man?

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.230
I’m not going to give you this position.

00:03:42.230 --> 00:03:44.620
I can’t…this position…you are better
than this.

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:46.870
I can’t do that to you.”

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.560
And that broke me for a second.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.420
I didn’t speak but I was like “What did
he say?”

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:55.090
And I went right back to listening to him
and he goes “Listen man this is the worst

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:56.090
position.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:58.690
I’ll tell you what, I want you to come and
meet the president of the company, he’s

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.690
got to meet you.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:01.460
Hang on one second.”

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.100
And he got up to leave and I’m like “Oh
my gosh?

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.100
What’s going on?”

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.510
He goes down and gets the president.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.051
He calls me and we walked down to the president’s
office and he introduces me to the guy and

00:04:11.051 --> 00:04:13.070
the guy is like “I hear great things about
you.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.640
We’d love to have you on board.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.479
I’ve given him full authority to give you
this route and this district and this territory.”

00:04:21.479 --> 00:04:22.689
And I’m like “Okay, great.

00:04:22.689 --> 00:04:23.710
Nice to meet you.”

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:27.740
They sat me down and offered me this really
great job.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:32.270
The first thing was like a $30,000 a year
job and I ended up with them offering me I

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:39.270
want to say $80,000 a year plus bonuses and
commission which would end up being like I

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.270
don’t know $150,000 a year maybe?

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.500
Maybe more?

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:48.419
This is 20-some odd years ago with a car,
with insurance, with a cellphone with a laptop,

00:04:48.419 --> 00:04:51.889
full benefits right out of the gate in one
of their best territories.

00:04:51.889 --> 00:04:55.490
Film Courage: Did you take it?

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.580
Dar: No.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:59.449
No and there was two reasons why.

00:04:59.449 --> 00:05:01.689
Number one I was stunned.

00:05:01.689 --> 00:05:04.580
I couldn’t believe that just happened.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:11.259
I was just doing this acting, listening exercise
that this is…wow?

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:14.729
And I was just kind of tripping because of
what had happened with what the actress and

00:05:14.729 --> 00:05:17.389
I when we were doing this exercise the night
before.

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:20.189
Both of us fully aware of what was going on.

00:05:20.189 --> 00:05:21.620
But she couldn’t help it.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:27.610
The same thing that happened to her on a much
more personal level happened in the room with

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:31.419
that gentleman (with Mr. X).

00:05:31.419 --> 00:05:35.169
And I just “Wow!”

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:36.169
And I thought about it.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.360
I was like “I can’t do that job.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.000
That’s a job-job man.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:41.520
Why am I going to go do this?

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.249
I can’t take time off and work.

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:46.840
I can’t go audition in the middle of the
day.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.400
I can’t do that.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:53.189
And so I respectfully declined and he said
“Listen, you are a winner!

00:05:53.189 --> 00:05:54.999
You’re on your way.”

00:05:54.999 --> 00:05:59.409
I didn’t say anything.

00:05:59.409 --> 00:06:04.681
I just listened to the man.

00:06:04.681 --> 00:06:08.999
Film Courage: Did you ever think about that
job if there were a few bumpy times and thought

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:13.590
“I should have taken it” or when there
were bumpy times were you like “I’m glad

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.520
I never took that job because that’s a full,
committed career?”

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.449
Dar: Well, yes it is.

00:06:19.449 --> 00:06:22.240
He was like “Listen, you’d have my job.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:23.240
I’m kind of glad.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:25.990
You’d have my job in 6 months if you were
here.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.069
I can see it in your eyes.”

00:06:27.069 --> 00:06:31.310
I’m like “What the heck is this guy talking
about?”

00:06:31.310 --> 00:06:35.810
Probably a mixture of both, back and forth.

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:37.699
When I was like “Oh my gosh!”

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:45.680
But ironically when I decided not to take
that job I just kind of went on a tear and

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:50.960
started working back to back to back to back
to back.

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.371
But it would have been nice in a lot of ways.

00:06:55.371 --> 00:07:03.580
I remember the class I was in that occurred
I mentioned to the guy who was teaching the

00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:12.710
class about that and he was a real…like…wrestling
with his own demons (that’s all I’ll say).

00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:18.940
He wasn’t almost the most pleasant cat which
was one of the reasons why I was like “I

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:20.039
can’t be with this guy.”

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:25.949
And it was kind of bittersweet because this
exercise that we done I thought this is so

00:07:25.949 --> 00:07:26.949
amazing.

00:07:26.949 --> 00:07:28.939
However, the personality leaves so much to
be desired.

00:07:28.939 --> 00:07:30.870
I don’t want to be around that anymore.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:37.539
I’m getting ahead of myself…so I told
the guy what had happened in this job interview

00:07:37.539 --> 00:07:42.780
four or five days before and he looks at me
and goes “Don’t do that.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.460
Stay in this business.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.120
You stay here and keep doing this.”

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.460
And that was like the nicest thing he’d
ever said.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:52.319
And I was like “Where the heck did that
come from?

00:07:52.319 --> 00:07:55.789
Thank you but where did that come from?”

00:07:55.789 --> 00:07:56.789
I don’t know.

00:07:56.789 --> 00:07:57.789
It was interesting.

00:07:57.789 --> 00:08:01.710
Film Courage: It’s interesting when you
have someone who doesn’t have to be nice

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:04.669
to you and they end up giving you a compliment.

00:08:04.669 --> 00:08:06.969
I think that might be a little more trustworthy.

00:08:06.969 --> 00:08:12.550
Dar: You are right but I think that guy should
be nice because he’s taking money from people

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:18.719
to provide a service and behaving opposite
of that.

00:08:18.719 --> 00:08:21.169
I’m not that guy.

00:08:21.169 --> 00:08:22.310
I’m not.

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:25.159
Film Courage: I hear you.

00:08:25.159 --> 00:08:28.219
Dar: I can’t be around that guy or that
gal.

